---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import GuideLayout from "../../components/GuideLayout.astro";
import { watershedCodeTheme } from "../../styles/code-theme";

const send = `import gleam/json
import watershed

// Broadcast which card this teammate is focused on. Fire-and-forget:
// nothing is stored, nothing is sequenced, no one converges on it.
pub fn announce_focus(doc, card_id: String) {
  watershed.submit_ripple(
    doc,
    ripple_type: "focus",
    content: json.object([#("card_id", json.string(card_id))]),
  )
}`;

const recv = `import gleam/dynamic/decode
import gleam/option.{Some}
import watershed

// React to every peer's ripples. \`ripple_client_id\` tells you who sent it;
// the content is left as Dynamic for you to decode per type.
watershed.subscribe_ripples(doc, fn(ripple) {
  case watershed.ripple_type(ripple) {
    Some("focus") -> {
      let decoded =
        decode.run(
          watershed.ripple_content(ripple),
          decode.at(["card_id"], decode.string),
        )
      case decoded, watershed.ripple_client_id(ripple) {
        Ok(card_id), Some(peer) -> move_peer_marker(peer, card_id)
        _, _ -> Nil
      }
    }
    Some("nudge") -> flash_nudge(ripple)
    _ -> Nil
  }
})`;
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="ripples"
  description="Step four of the watershed build guide: ripples send document-scoped, non-sequenced broadcasts to other connected clients. Use them for live cursors, presence, and reactions that do not belong in durable document state."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Keep temporary signals outside the document</h2>
    <p>
      Some signals have short validity. Examples include the card that a
      teammate views, a cursor position, a request for attention, or a reaction.
      Do not store these signals in a channel. A channel sequences, summarizes,
      and replays its data to later clients.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <strong>ripple</strong> is a document-scoped broadcast without
      sequencing. watershed sends it to each other connected client and does
      not store it. Ripples do not require ordering or convergence.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Send a ripple</h2>
    <p>
      A ripple is a <code>type</code> tag plus a JSON payload.
      <code>submit_ripple</code> broadcasts it and returns nothing. There is no
      state change and no acknowledgment. Flowboard announces the
      card a teammate is focused on:
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={send} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Receive ripples</h2>
    <p>
      <code>subscribe_ripples</code> hands every inbound ripple to your handler.
      Match on <code>ripple_type</code>, decode <code>ripple_content</code> at
      the boundary, and use <code>ripple_client_id</code> to know which peer it
      came from. Use these fields to render a live roster of each peer's current
      location.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={recv} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>Use ripples for temporary signals and channels for state.</strong>
        A summary does not contain ripples, so a later client cannot receive
        them. Store data in a channel if later clients must receive it.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the BEAM, <code>subscribe_ripples(doc)</code> returns a
        <code>Subject(Ripple)</code>. Receive ripples from that subject. The
        browser facade takes a callback.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Track connected clients with presence</h2>
    <p>
      Ripples do not report when a peer disconnects, so its last marker can
      remain on screen. <strong>Presence</strong> manages this lifecycle. A
      server with connection-backed presence tracks each socket. A later client
      receives the complete roster, and a dropped connection removes its entry.
      Other servers use ripple heartbeats and a TTL. One API supports both
      modes and reports the selected mode. The next step adds the presence driver
      to the user interface as one effect.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keep sending high-frequency cursor motion as raw ripples; presence is for
      membership and the slower status that goes with it.
    </p>
    <p>
      For details about both implementations, heartbeat timing, fallback TTL,
      and reconnection, read the runtime note on{" "}
      <a href="/runtime/presence">presence and ripples</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">You can now</span>
    <p>
      Broadcast temporary signals such as cursors, focus, and reactions to each
      other client without changing the document’s converged state. Decode each
      signal at the input boundary.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
